Gateway HTTPS policy on custom ports
====================================

When verify_ssl is true, OpenWRT and automatic MikroTik transport selection
use HTTPS regardless of the configured port. A nonstandard HTTPS listener
no longer silently becomes an HTTP connection. An HTTP-only endpoint fails
before credential-bearing HTTP; there is no automatic insecure fallback.

MikroTik and pfSense constructors reject the contradictory combination
use_ssl=false and verify_ssl=true before constructing an HTTP client.
Correct the connection settings to select HTTPS and a trusted certificate.
An intentionally insecure legacy connection requires verify_ssl=false.
That explicit opt-out preserves prior port/protocol selection: OpenWRT and
automatic MikroTik use their previous port heuristics, while explicit
MikroTik/pfSense use_ssl choices remain effective. OPNsense remains HTTPS.
This patch does not globally change low-level constructor default values.

Existing database rows are not rewritten. However, a saved verified custom-
port OpenWRT/MikroTik connection now actually requires HTTPS. Operators who
previously relied on accidental plaintext must configure TLS or deliberately
disable verification. Verified explicit-HTTP settings are now refused.
No schema migration, dependency change or device configuration write occurs.

Qualification includes actual HTTPX and vendor clients against isolated
HTTP-only and private-CA TLS peers. The harness relocates only the validated
fixture hostname to loopback; it preserves the selected scheme and actual
custom port. Trusted controls alone inject the fixture CA. Appliance payloads
are synthetic and the probe closes its own clients. These checks do not
establish hardware interoperability, general SSRF safety, custom trust-store
installation. Standalone service cleanup is qualified separately in
GATEWAY-TEST-LIFECYCLE, including its best-effort cleanup limits.
